# KNOWN GAP (found R45 Track G while auditing the chain-link postfix axis).
#
# The REFERENCE LAGS THE SELF-HOST here — a succession milestone, not an
# embarrassment. TWO Rust-lane defects in one program:
#
#   (1) `.is_none()` is accepted on a `Result`, and ANSWERS `false`. `is_none`
#       is an Option tag test; a Result has no None arm, so the answer is
#       meaningless rather than merely surprising. The self-host REJECTS it:
#       `error[E_NoMethodFound]: `.is_none()` on Result is outside the phase-0
#       subset (Option-only)`.
#
#   (2) `s.fetch()!` — a postfix error-propagation mark — is accepted inside
#       `Ok(...)` in a function that is NOT declared `throws`. The identical
#       shape in a plain binding IS rejected, with
#       `error[E_UnhandledThrows]: this call can fail with `String` but the
#       error is not handled here`. So the enforcement has a hole at the
#       enum-constructor argument position.
#
# CORRECT behaviour: `gg check` REJECTS this program on both lanes — (1) as
# a no-such-method error and (2) as E_UnhandledThrows. It currently prints
# `false` on the Rust lane, which is the worst outcome: a wrong answer with
# rc 0.
struct Src:
    int n

equip Src:
    Result[Option[int], String] fetch(self):
        return Ok(None())

Result[bool, String] go(Src s):
    return Ok(s.fetch()!.is_none())

void main():
    Src s = Src(1)
    print(go(s).unwrap())
